__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
.env
.env.*

# Created locally by the docker-compose.yml bind mounts (state/, data/,
# output/) or by running the SQLite-backed store directly -- never
# meant to be committed. Mirrors .dockerignore's own exclusions.
#
# data/ holds downloaded market history (see `cli.py fetch-data`) --
# tens of MB per symbol-year. It was missing from this list until now
# even though the comment above always claimed to cover it; the
# directory stayed untracked only because it happened to be empty.
state/
data/
output/
*.db

# fidelity-api writes several files into the CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY,
# some of which are credential-equivalent. Listed here as defense in
# depth -- the real fix is passing save_state=False / an explicit
# profile_path outside the repo, and never debug=True (see
# src/fidelity_broker.py). This list exists so that a mistake there is
# still not committable.
#
#   Fidelity.json / Fidelity_<title>.json
#       Playwright storage_state: the complete cookie + localStorage set
#       for an AUTHENTICATED brokerage session, plaintext. Written
#       unconditionally by close_browser() when save_state is left at
#       its default of True. With save_device=True it also carries
#       Fidelity's 2FA-bypass token.
#   fidelity_trace*.zip
#       Playwright trace, written when debug=True. Records .fill()
#       arguments -- i.e. the USERNAME AND PASSWORD in cleartext --
#       plus screenshots and DOM snapshots.
#   Statements/
#       Downloaded account statements.
#   Portfolio_Positions*.csv
#       The positions export. Note the library uses the SERVER-supplied
#       download.suggested_filename, so this pattern matches Fidelity's
#       current naming and is not a guarantee; the adapter is
#       responsible for cleaning it up rather than relying on this.
Fidelity.json
Fidelity_*.json
fidelity_trace*.zip
Statements/
Portfolio_Positions*.csv

# fidelity_recon.py's traffic dumps. Written to ~/.fidelity_recon by
# default -- outside the repo entirely -- but --artifact-dir can point
# anywhere, so the pattern is listed here too. The dump holds captured
# traffic from an AUTHENTICATED session: session tokens and account
# numbers. TrafficCapture scrubs the literal credential values and
# redacts secret-looking JSON keys, which is mitigation, not elimination.
.fidelity_recon/
recon_*.json

# HAR exports from browser DevTools. A HAR of an authenticated session
# contains live Cookie/Authorization headers -- credential-equivalent
# for a brokerage login -- plus account numbers and balances. Never
# committable. See analyze_har.py, which reads these and offers
# --redact to produce a scrubbed copy.
*.har

# Runtime logs from the paper/live supervisor. Machine-local by nature:
# they record one deployment's session on one host, so committing them
# creates conflicts between machines that are trading independently and
# says nothing either of them needs. They also carry account balances
# and position sizes in the loop's own output.
logs/
